course: dictionary
lesson: 10178
title: Tight: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it show you which one is meant. «Tight» can mean fitting closely, pulled firmly, held strongly, or with very little to spare. Read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: «In Baltimore, Tremaine's dad does his cornrows (tight braids along the head) before school.» The brackets give the clue: tight braids lie close along the head. Brackets, examples and explanations like this are often placed right next to a hard word to help you.
explain: «Loose garden netting can trap birds and hedgehogs, so it is kept tight or taken away.» Here tight is the opposite of loose. Netting that is pulled firmly is safe, and netting that hangs loose is not. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
fact: Tight can be an adjective (tight, tighter, tightest), an adverb (tightly), or a verb (tighten).
fact: «Tight» means fitting very closely, with little space: tight shoes, a tight lid.
fact: «Tight» means pulled or stretched firmly: a tight rope.
fact: «Tight» means held strongly, as in «hold on tight».
fact: «Tight» can mean with very little time or money to spare, as in a tight schedule. A tight game is very close, with nobody far ahead.
exercise: Keeping open food in tight boxes stops mice and insects from getting at it. What does tight mean here? | Loose and wide open | *Fitting closely, with no gaps for mice to get in | Painted in bright colours | Very heavy and hard to lift
exercise: Hold on tight on the swing. What does tight mean? | Very quietly | Without touching anything | Loosely, so you can drop it | *Firmly, so you do not let go
exercise: A tight game is one where | *nobody is far ahead and the score is very close | one team has scored many more points | nobody plays at all | the game is played in a very small room
test: Sam had a tight schedule, with no time to spare. What does tight mean? | Covered in mud | *With very little time to spare | With lots of free time to play | Very slow and lazy
test: What does tight mean in «a tight rope»? | Thick and fluffy | Full of water | *Pulled or stretched firmly | Very far away
exam: In which sentence does tight mean held strongly? | The night was cold and dark | The lid was loose and the box was empty | *Please hold on tight to the rail | The river was wide and calm
